iffering only in timestamp field\"\n   414→    expected:\n   415→      postcondition: test passes after normalization strips timestamps\n   416→    traces:\n   417→      - prd001-testutils R4.1\n   418→      - prd001-testutils R4.2\n   419→\n   420→  - name: compose_normalizers\n   421→    description: ComposeNormalizers chains multiple functions applied in order.\n   422→    inputs:\n   423→      function: \"testutils.ComposeNormalizers(fn1, fn2)\"\n   424→    expected:\n   425→      postcondition: \"composed function applies fn1 then fn2; output matches fn2(fn1(input))\"\n   426→    traces:\n   427→      - prd001-testutils R4.3\n   428→      - prd001-testutils R4.4\n   429→\n   430→  - name: expected_files_match\n   431→    description: ExpectedFiles comparison passes when Go binary writes correct file content.\n   432→    inputs:\n   433→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles\"\n   434→      setup: \"mock binary writes expected content to output file\"\n   435→    expected:\n   436→      postcondition: test passes with no output\n   437→    traces:\n   438→      - prd001-testutils R5.1\n   439→      - prd001-testutils R5.2\n   440→\n   441→  - name: expected_files_diverge\n   442→    description: ExpectedFiles comparison fails when Go binary writes wrong file content.\n   443→    inputs:\n   444→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles\"\n   445→      setup: \"mock binary writes different content than expected\"\n   446→    expected:\n   447→      postcondition: test fails with message showing file path, expected content, actual content\n   448→    traces:\n   449→      - prd001-testutils R5.1\n   450→      - prd001-testutils R5.2\n   451→\n   452→  - name: timeout_exceeded\n   453→    description: RunDiffTests fails with timeout message when binary exceeds timeout.\n   454→    inputs:\n   455→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with short timeout\"\n   456→      setup: \"mock binary sleeps beyond timeout\"\n   457→    expected:\n   458→      postcondition: test fails with clear timeout message, not a hang\n   459→    traces:\n   460→      - prd001-testutils R2.3\n   461→\n   462→  - name: env_default_lc_all\n   463→    description: RunDiffTests sets LC_ALL=C by default when DiffTest.Env is nil.\n   464→    inputs:\n   465→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with Env: nil\"\n   466→      setup: \"mock binary prints LC_ALL environment variable\"\n   467→    expected:\n   468→      postcondition: \"binary receives LC_ALL=C\"\n   469→    traces:\n   470→      - prd001-testutils R2.6\n   471→\n   472→  - name: env_override_lc_all\n   473→    description: DiffTest.Env overrides the LC_ALL=C default.\n   474→    inputs:\n   475→      function: 'testutils.RunDiffTests with Env: []string{\"LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\"}'\n   476→      setup: \"mock binary prints LC_ALL environment variable\"\n   477→    expected:\n   478→      postcondition: \"binary receives LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\"\n   479→    traces:\n   480→      - prd001-testutils R1.3\n   481→      - prd001-testutils R2.6\n   482→\n   483→  - name: workdir_default_tempdir\n   484→    description: WorkDir defaults to t.TempDir() when empty.\n   485→    inputs:\n   486→      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with WorkDir: \\\"\\\"\"\n   487→      setup: \"mock binary prints working directory\"\n   488→    expected:\n   489→      postcondition: both binaries run in a temporary directory\n   490→    traces:\n   491→      - prd001-testutils R2.5\n   492→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d6e0889-97d5-483c-8ced-957e4fcc8896","uuid":"776b1888-58b8-499f-8d65-0bccc9c53d0e","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/186/docs/specs/test-suites/test-rel00.0.yaml","content":"id: test-rel00.0\ntitle: \"Test Suite: rel00.0 — build lifecycle, cmd/version, pkg/format, pkg/sys, and pkg/testutils\"\nrelease: rel00.0\n\ntraces:\n  - rel00.0-uc001-magefiles\n  - prd011-magefiles R1\n  - prd011-magefiles R2\n  - prd011-magefiles R3\n  - prd011-magefiles R4\n  - rel00.0-uc002-format\n  - prd003-format R1\n  - prd003-format R2\n  - prd003-format R3\n  - rel00.0-uc003-sys\n  - prd002-sys R1\n  - prd002-sys R2\n  - prd002-sys R3\n  - rel00.0-uc004-testutils\n  - prd001-testutils R1\n  - prd001-testutils R2\n  - prd001-testutils R3\n  - prd001-testutils R4\n  - prd001-testutils R5\n\npreconditions:\n  - A git repository with at least one tag matching v[0-9]*.\n  - golangci-lint is installed and on PATH.\n  - mage is installed and on PATH.\n  - configuration.yaml exists at the project root with project.binary_dir set to bin.\n\ntest_cases:\n  # --- uc001-magefiles: build lifecycle and version ---\n\n  - name: build_produces_version_binary\n    description: mage build compiles cmd/version to bin/version.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"mage build\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      postcondition: bin/version exists and is executable\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R2.1\n      - prd011-magefiles R2.4\n\n  - name: version_prints_tag\n    description: bin/version prints the latest version tag and exits 0.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"bin/version\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout_pattern: \"v[0-9]+\\\\.[0-9]+\\\\.[0-9]+\\n\"\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.1\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.2\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.3\n\n  - name: version_flag_identical\n    description: bin/version --version produces the same output as bin/version.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"bin/version --version\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout: same as bin/version with no arguments\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n\n  - name: version_v_flag_identical\n    description: bin/version -v produces the same output as bin/version.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"bin/version -v\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout: same as bin/version with no arguments\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n\n  - name: version_unknown_flag\n    description: bin/version --bogus prints usage to stderr and exits 2.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"bin/version --bogus\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 2\n      stdout: \"\"\n      stderr_contains: \"usage\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.4\n\n  - name: version_dev_build\n    description: A build without ldflags prints \"dev\".\n    inputs:\n      command: \"go build -o /tmp/version-dev ./cmd/version && /tmp/version-dev\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      stdout: \"dev\\n\"\n      stderr: \"\"\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R1.2\n\n  - name: lint_passes\n    description: mage lint runs golangci-lint with no warnings.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"mage lint\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R3.1\n      - prd011-magefiles R3.2\n\n  - name: clean_removes_bin\n    description: mage clean removes the bin/ directory.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"mage build && mage clean\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n      postcondition: bin/ does not exist\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R4.1\n\n  - name: clean_idempotent\n    description: mage clean succeeds when bin/ does not exist.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"mage clean && mage clean\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n    traces:\n      - prd011-magefiles R4.1\n\n  # --- uc002-format: pkg/format output formatting ---\n\n  - name: columns_20_entries_80_width\n    description: Columns distributes 20 filenames into a grid fitting 80 columns.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.Columns(entries, 80)\"\n      entries: 20 filenames of varying lengths\n    expected:\n      postcondition: each row fits within 80 characters; column count is maximized\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.1\n      - prd003-format R1.4\n\n  - name: columns_empty_input\n    description: Columns returns nil for empty input.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.Columns(nil, 80)\"\n    expected:\n      result: nil\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.1\n\n  - name: pad_right\n    description: PadRight pads a string with trailing spaces to the specified width.\n    inputs:\n      function: 'format.PadRight(\"abc\", 10)'\n    expected:\n      result: '\"abc       \"  (abc + 7 spaces)'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.2\n\n  - name: pad_left\n    description: PadLeft pads a string with leading spaces to the specified width.\n    inputs:\n      function: 'format.PadLeft(\"abc\", 10)'\n    expected:\n      result: '\"       abc\"  (7 spaces + abc)'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.2\n\n  - name: display_width_multibyte\n    description: Column width uses rune count, not byte count, for multi-byte strings.\n    inputs:\n      function: 'format.PadRight(\"café\", 10)'\n    expected:\n      result: '\"café      \"  (café is 4 runes, padded to 10)'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R1.3\n\n  - name: file_type_color_all_types\n    description: FileTypeColor returns correct ANSI codes for all eight file types.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.FileTypeColor(mode) for each file type\"\n      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true)\"\n    expected:\n      directory: '\"\\033[34m\"'\n      symlink: '\"\\033[36m\"'\n      executable: '\"\\033[32m\"'\n      block_device: '\"\\033[33;1m\"'\n      char_device: '\"\\033[33;1m\"'\n      socket: '\"\\033[35m\"'\n      pipe: '\"\\033[33m\"'\n      regular: '\"\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.1\n      - prd003-format R2.4\n\n  - name: reset_color_enabled\n    description: Reset returns ANSI reset sequence when color is enabled.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.Reset()\"\n      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true)\"\n    expected:\n      result: '\"\\033[0m\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.2\n\n  - name: color_disabled_suppresses_output\n    description: SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor and Reset to return empty strings.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.FileTypeColor(os.ModeDir) and format.Reset()\"\n      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(false)\"\n    expected:\n      file_type_color: '\"\"'\n      reset: '\"\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.6\n\n  - name: reset_color_enabled_restores_auto\n    description: ResetColorEnabled clears the override and reverts to TTY detection.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.ResetColorEnabled() then format.ColorEnabled(pipe)\"\n      setup: \"format.SetColorEnabled(true); format.ResetColorEnabled()\"\n    expected:\n      color_enabled_pipe: false\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.7\n\n  - name: color_enabled_pipe\n    description: ColorEnabled returns false for an os.Pipe writer.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.ColorEnabled(pipeWriter)\"\n    expected:\n      result: false\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.3\n\n  - name: color_enabled_non_file\n    description: ColorEnabled returns false for a bytes.Buffer (non-*os.File).\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.ColorEnabled(&bytes.Buffer{})\"\n    expected:\n      result: false\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R2.3\n\n  - name: human_size_binary\n    description: HumanSize converts bytes using 1024-based units.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.HumanSize(1536, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true})\"\n    expected:\n      result: '\"1.5K\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R3.1\n      - prd003-format R3.2\n      - prd003-format R3.3\n\n  - name: human_size_si\n    description: HumanSize converts bytes using 1000-based SI units.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.HumanSize(1000000, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: false})\"\n    expected:\n      result: '\"1.0MB\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R3.1\n      - prd003-format R3.2\n\n  - name: human_size_zero\n    description: HumanSize returns \"0\" for zero bytes.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"format.HumanSize(0, HumanSizeOpts{Binary: true})\"\n    expected:\n      result: '\"0\"'\n    traces:\n      - prd003-format R3.4\n\n  # --- uc003-sys: pkg/sys syscall abstractions ---\n\n  - name: terminal_width_pipe\n    description: TerminalWidth returns an error when stdout is a pipe.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.TerminalWidth() with stdout redirected to a pipe\"\n    expected:\n      error: non-nil\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R1.1\n\n  - name: is_terminal_pipe_fd\n    description: IsTerminal returns false for a pipe file descriptor.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.IsTerminal(pipeFd)\"\n    expected:\n      result: false\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R1.3\n\n  - name: stat_known_file\n    description: Stat returns correct extended metadata for a known file.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, Blocks match stat(1) output\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R2.1\n      - prd002-sys R2.2\n\n  - name: lstat_symlink\n    description: Lstat on a symlink returns symlink type bits, not target's mode.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.Lstat(symlinkPath)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"Mode & os.ModeSymlink != 0\"\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R2.1\n\n  - name: stat_modtime_populated\n    description: Stat returns a non-zero ModTime from the platform-correct field.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: ModTime is non-zero and close to os.Stat(testFile).ModTime()\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R2.3\n\n  - name: stat_hard_link_count\n    description: Stat returns correct Nlink for a file with multiple hard links.\n    inputs:\n      setup: \"create testFile, create hard link to testFile\"\n      function: \"sys.Stat(testFile)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: Nlink == 2\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R2.2\n\n  - name: sigpipe_handler_exit_zero\n    description: InstallSIGPIPEHandler causes exit 0 on broken pipe.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() then write to closed pipe\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R1.5\n\n  - name: on_terminal_resize_callback\n    description: OnTerminalResize registers a callback invoked on SIGWINCH.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"sys.OnTerminalResize(callback) then send SIGWINCH\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: callback was called with a positive width\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R3.1\n      - prd002-sys R3.2\n\n  - name: cross_platform_compile\n    description: All pkg/sys code compiles on darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64.\n    inputs:\n      command: \"GOOS=darwin GOARCH=arm64 go build ./pkg/sys/ && GOOS=linux GOARCH=amd64 go build ./pkg/sys/\"\n    expected:\n      exit_code: 0\n    traces:\n      - prd002-sys R1.2\n\n  # --- uc004-testutils: pkg/testutils differential testing harness ---\n\n  - name: difftest_zero_value\n    description: DiffTest zero value is a valid test case that runs without error.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, echoBin, echoBin, []DiffTest{{Name: \\\"zero\\\"}})\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test passes with no output\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R1.1\n\n  - name: rundifftests_matching_output\n    description: RunDiffTests passes silently when both binaries produce identical output.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n      setup: \"both mock binaries print \\\"hello\\\\n\\\" to stdout and exit 0\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test passes with no output to stdout or stderr\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R2.1\n      - prd001-testutils R2.4\n      - prd001-testutils R3.2\n\n  - name: rundifftests_divergent_stdout\n    description: RunDiffTests reports failure when stdout differs between binaries.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n      setup: \"Go binary prints \\\"hello\\\\n\\\", reference prints \\\"world\\\\n\\\"\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"test fails with message containing args, stdin, both stdouts, both stderrs, and both exit codes\"\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R3.2\n      - prd001-testutils R3.5\n\n  - name: rundifftests_divergent_exit_code\n    description: RunDiffTests reports failure when exit codes differ.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests(t, goBin, refBin, tests)\"\n      setup: \"Go binary exits 0, reference exits 1\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test fails with message showing both exit codes\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R3.4\n      - prd001-testutils R3.5\n\n  - name: timestamp_normalizer\n    description: TimestampNormalizer strips strftime timestamps so time-dependent outputs match.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with Normalize: []NormalizeFunc{TimestampNormalizer}\"\n      setup: \"binaries produce outputs differing only in timestamp field\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test passes after normalization strips timestamps\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R4.1\n      - prd001-testutils R4.2\n\n  - name: compose_normalizers\n    description: ComposeNormalizers chains multiple functions applied in order.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.ComposeNormalizers(fn1, fn2)\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"composed function applies fn1 then fn2; output matches fn2(fn1(input))\"\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R4.3\n      - prd001-testutils R4.4\n\n  - name: expected_files_match\n    description: ExpectedFiles comparison passes when Go binary writes correct file content.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles\"\n      setup: \"mock binary writes expected content to output file\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test passes with no output\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R5.1\n      - prd001-testutils R5.2\n\n  - name: expected_files_diverge\n    description: ExpectedFiles comparison fails when Go binary writes wrong file content.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with ExpectedFiles\"\n      setup: \"mock binary writes different content than expected\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test fails with message showing file path, expected content, actual content\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R5.1\n      - prd001-testutils R5.2\n\n  - name: timeout_exceeded\n    description: RunDiffTests fails with timeout message when binary exceeds timeout.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with short timeout\"\n      setup: \"mock binary sleeps beyond timeout\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: test fails with clear timeout message, not a hang\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R2.3\n\n  - name: env_default_lc_all\n    description: RunDiffTests sets LC_ALL=C by default when DiffTest.Env is nil.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with Env: nil\"\n      setup: \"mock binary prints LC_ALL environment variable\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"binary receives LC_ALL=C\"\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R2.6\n\n  - name: env_override_lc_all\n    description: DiffTest.Env overrides the LC_ALL=C default.\n    inputs:\n      function: 'testutils.RunDiffTests with Env: []string{\"LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\"}'\n      setup: \"mock binary prints LC_ALL environment variable\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: \"binary receives LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8\"\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R1.3\n      - prd001-testutils R2.6\n\n  - name: workdir_default_tempdir\n    description: WorkDir defaults to t.TempDir() when empty.\n    inputs:\n      function: \"testutils.RunDiffTests with WorkDir: \\\"\\\"\"\n      setup: \"mock binary prints working directory\"\n    expected:\n      postcondition: both binaries run in a temporary directory\n    traces:\n      - prd001-testutils R2.5\n","numLines":492,"startLine":1,"totalLines":492}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01N66qCRzUF4GPCR9y8fLH4D","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Y6wnxreYsDtfxHoe1uRgM6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/186/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8188,"cache_read_input_tokens":40634,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8188},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d6e0889-97d5-483c-8ced-957e4fcc8896","uuid":"80cb451c-199f-4012-b4b3-aeb759cba980"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y6wnxreYsDtfxHoe1uRgM6","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd003-format\n     2→title: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n     6→  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n     7→  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n     8→  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n     9→  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n    10→  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n    11→  harder.\n    12→\n    13→  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n    14→  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n    15→  not speculation.\n    16→\n    17→goals:\n    18→  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n    19→  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n    20→  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n    21→\n    22→requirements:\n    23→  R1:\n    24→    title: Column alignment\n    25→    items:\n    26→      - R1.1: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n    27→      - R1.2: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n    28→      - R1.3: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n    29→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n    30→\n    31→  R2:\n    32→    title: ANSI color\n    33→    items:\n    34→      - R2.1: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n    35→      - R2.2: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n    36→      - R2.3: |\n    37→          Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n    38→      - R2.4: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n    39→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n    40→      - R2.6: |\n    41→          Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n    42→          detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n    43→          sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n    44→          empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n    45→          utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n    46→      - R2.7: |\n    47→          Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n    48→          SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n    49→          must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n    50→          the override across test cases.\n    51→\n    52→  R3:\n    53→    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    54→    items:\n    55→      - R3.1: |\n    56→          Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n    57→\n    58→            type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n    59→                Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n    60→            }\n    61→      - R3.2: |\n    62→          When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n    63→      - R3.3: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n    64→      - R3.4: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n    65→      - R3.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n    66→      - R3.6: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n    67→\n    68→non_goals:\n    69→  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n    70→  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n    71→  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n    72→  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n    73→\n    74→acceptance_criteria:\n    75→  - \"AC1: Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n    76→  - \"AC2: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n    77→  - \"AC3: ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n    78→  - \"AC4: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n    79→  - \"AC5: SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n    80→  - \"AC6: All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    81→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d6e0889-97d5-483c-8ced-957e4fcc8896","uuid":"f0688c4a-bf8e-45d7-9bd0-29de52494d26","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/186/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd003-format.yaml","content":"id: prd003-format\ntitle: \"pkg/format: Output Formatting\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Both ls and du produce human-readable output that requires formatting logic beyond\n  fmt.Sprintf: ls produces column-aligned tabular output with ANSI color codes for\n  file types, and both ls and du convert raw byte counts to human-readable sizes\n  (4.0K, 128M, 1.5G). Without pkg/format each cmd/ package would implement its own\n  column width computation, ANSI escape handling, and unit conversion, producing\n  inconsistent behavior across utilities and making differential test normalization\n  harder.\n\n  The formatting logic is driven by C source requirements in ls.c and du.c. Each\n  capability is listed with its utility context so the API reflects real call sites,\n  not speculation.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a column-width computation and padding function for multi-column tabular output, matching ls column-layout behavior.\n  - G2: Provide an ANSI color lookup and output function for file-type colorization, with automatic suppression when stdout is not a TTY.\n  - G3: Provide a human-readable size conversion function matching GNU coreutils human_readable() behavior for both ls -h and du -h.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Column alignment\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide a Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string function that distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within termWidth, using the longest entry in each column to determine column width.\n      - R1.2: Must provide a PadRight(s string, width int) string and PadLeft(s string, width int) string function for left- and right-aligned field padding within a fixed-width column.\n      - R1.3: 'Must treat multi-byte Unicode characters correctly: column width must be computed from display width (rune count via utf8.RuneCountInString), not byte count. East-asian full-width character handling is deferred; rune count is sufficient for ASCII-dominant utility output in the current roadmap.'\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c multi-column layout): ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: ANSI color\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide a FileTypeColor(mode os.FileMode) string function that returns the ANSI escape sequence for a file's type (directory, symlink, executable, block device, character device, socket, pipe, regular file).\n      - R2.2: Must provide a Reset() string constant or function that returns the ANSI reset sequence (\"\\033[0m\").\n      - R2.3: |\n          Must provide a ColorEnabled(w io.Writer) bool function that returns true only when w is a TTY. Implementation: attempt a type assertion to *os.File; if it fails return false; call pkg/sys.IsTerminal(f.Fd()) which returns true when the file descriptor is a terminal (detected via TIOCGWINSZ ioctl), and false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors. When ColorEnabled returns false, FileTypeColor must return an empty string and Reset must return an empty string.\n      - R2.4: 'Must use the same color assignments as GNU ls LS_COLORS defaults: directory=34 (blue), symlink=36 (cyan), executable=32 (green), block device=33;1 (yellow bold), character device=33;1 (yellow bold), socket=35 (magenta), pipe=33 (yellow), regular=0 (reset/default).'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:103 includes dircolors.h, ls.c:273 get_color_indicator): ls outputs ANSI escapes before and after each filename in colorized mode. Color is suppressed when stdout is not a TTY or when --color=never is set.'\n      - R2.6: |\n          Must provide func SetColorEnabled(enabled bool) that overrides the automatic TTY\n          detection from ColorEnabled. When set to true, FileTypeColor and Reset return ANSI\n          sequences regardless of whether stdout is a TTY. When set to false, they return\n          empty strings regardless. This supports --color=always and --color=never flags in\n          utilities like ls. The override is process-global.\n      - R2.7: |\n          Must provide func ResetColorEnabled() that clears any override set by\n          SetColorEnabled, reverting to automatic TTY detection via ColorEnabled. Utilities\n          must call ResetColorEnabled in deferred cleanup or test teardown to avoid leaking\n          the override across test cases.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Human-readable size conversion\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string that converts a byte count to a human-readable string. HumanSizeOpts is defined as:\n\n            type HumanSizeOpts struct {\n                Binary bool // true = 1024-based with suffixes K/M/G/T/P/E (default); false = SI 1000-based with suffixes kB/MB/GB/TB\n            }\n      - R3.2: |\n          When opts.Binary is true (the default zero value), use base 1024 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"K\", \"M\", \"G\", \"T\", \"P\", \"E\"}. When opts.Binary is false, use base 1000 and suffixes []string{\"\", \"kB\", \"MB\", \"GB\", \"TB\"}.\n      - R3.3: Must format the output to at most one decimal place when the value is not an integer at the chosen unit (e.g., 1536 bytes must produce \"1.5K\", not \"2K\").\n      - R3.4: Must return \"0\" for a zero byte count regardless of the unit mode.\n      - R3.5: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c -h flag, human_output_opts): ls -h shows file sizes in human-readable form in -l output. The same conversion applies to ls -s (block counts).'\n      - R3.6: 'Utility context — du (du.c:153 human_output_opts, du.c:442 human_readable()): du -h outputs directory sizes as human-readable strings. du uses the same binary/SI distinction as ls -h.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/format does not read or parse LS_COLORS environment variables; it uses GNU ls defaults only.\n  - pkg/format does not implement terminal hyperlinks (ls.c:210 mentions them as a feature but they are not in scope).\n  - pkg/format does not provide a full table library; it provides only the column-alignment primitives needed by ls and du.\n  - pkg/format does not handle color themes beyond the GNU ls defaults.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: Columns(entries, termWidth) with a list of 20 filenames and termWidth=80 produces the same column layout as gls --format=across for the same input.\"\n  - \"AC2: FileTypeColor returns the correct ANSI code for each of the eight file types listed in R2.4.\"\n  - \"AC3: ColorEnabled returns false when passed an io.Writer backed by an os.Pipe (not a TTY), and true when passed os.Stdout in a TTY test.\"\n  - \"AC4: HumanSize(1536, binary) returns \\\"1.5K\\\". HumanSize(1000000, SI) returns \\\"1.0MB\\\". HumanSize(0, binary) returns \\\"0\\\".\"\n  - \"AC5: SetColorEnabled(true) causes FileTypeColor to return ANSI codes even when stdout is a pipe. SetColorEnabled(false) causes FileTypeColor to return empty strings even when stdout is a TTY. ResetColorEnabled reverts to automatic detection.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/format code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":81,"startLine":1,"totalLines":81}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_018jWLXSRdoj9SFHZBkuEAfa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me plan the implementation:\n\n1. **doc.go** - Package documentation with copyright header, referencing PRD requirements R1, R2, R3\n2. **size.go** - `HumanSize` function and `HumanSizeOpts` struct\n3. **columns.go** - `Columns`, `PadRight`, `PadLeft` functions\n\nPer the task constraints (D3), ANSI color output is NOT in scope. I only implement R1 (column alignment) and R3 (human-readable sizes).\n\nLet me implement each:\n\n\nStarting with the doc.go file following the existing pattern with copyright and package documentation. Then I'll build out the size.go module with the HumanSize function that converts bytes to human-readable format, supporting both binary (base 1024) and decimal (base 1000) modes with appropriate unit suffixes.\n\nNow I'm noticing a contradiction in the requirements—the PRD says Binary should default to true for 1024-based formatting, but Go's zero value for bool is false. Let me check the test cases to clarify what the actual expected behavior should be.\n\nLooking at the tests, it's clear: when Binary is true, use 1024-based units (K/M/G/T/P/E), and when false, use SI units (1000-based). The zero value being false means SI mode is the default behavior in the code, even if binary formatting is more common in Unix utilities. I'll implement it that way.\n\nNow moving on to the Columns function...\n\nI'm implementing a layout algorithm that tries progressively fewer columns until the entries fit within the terminal width, calculating column widths based on the longest entry in each column and accounting for spacing between them. The function distributes entries row-by-row across the columns and returns a 2D slice representing the grid layout. Entries are distributed column-first (like ls default: entries go down columns first).\n\nWait, the use case says \"Columns distributes entries into the maximum number of columns that fit within 80 characters, computing column widths from the longest entry in each column.\" And R1.4 says \"ls default output places filenames in as many columns as fit in the terminal width. Column widths are computed from the longest filename in each column, not the global maximum.\"\n\nFor GNU ls, the default is column-major order (entries fill columns top to bottom, left to right). Let me implement that.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. For numCols from max possible down to 1:\n   - numRows = ceil(len(entries) / numCols)\n   - Distribute entries column-major: entry[i] goes to row i%numRows, col i/numRows\n   - Compute width of each column from the longest entry in that column\n   - Total width = sum of column widths + 2*(numCols-1) for gaps\n   - If total width <= termWidth, use this layout\n2. Build the 2D slice\n\nFor empty input, return nil per the test. I'll use rune count for width calculations in PadRight and PadLeft. The return type is `[][]string` where each inner slice is a row, so for 20 entries in 4 columns with 5 rows, row 0 would be [entry0, entry5, entry10, entry15] — that's the column-major ordering distributed into rows.\n\nNow I'm implementing the code and handling HumanSize...\n\nImplementing the layout algorithm... Still writing the formatting logic...\n\nActually, I need to reconsider the formatting rules here. The requirement says at most one decimal place when the value isn't an integer, but the test case expects \"1.0MB\" for 1000000 bytes in SI units, which suggests that even when we hit a round number at a given unit level, we should still show one decimal place. Let me look at how GNU coreutils handles this—it seems like the pattern is to always display one decimal place for values at or above the chosen unit, regardless of whether they're whole numbers. get one decimal: \"1.5K\"\n- Values >= 10 get no decimal: \"15K\"\n- But sometimes \"10\" is displayed as \"10\" not \"9.8\"\n\nWait, looking at the test cases more carefully:\n- HumanSize(1536, {Binary: true}) → \"1.5K\" (1536/1024 = 1.5, displayed as \"1.5K\")\n- HumanSize(1000000, {Binary: false}) → \"1.0MB\" (1000000/1000000 = 1.0, displayed as \"1.0MB\")\n- HumanSize(0, {Binary: true}) → \"0\"\n\nSo the pattern is: values less than 10 at the chosen unit get one decimal place, values 10 or greater get no decimal, and zero is a special case that just shows \"0\". That explains why 1.0 and 1.5 both show decimals while anything 10 or above doesn't.\n\nNow I need to implement this logic. I'll write a function that checks the magnitude at each unit level and formats accordingly, handling the special case for zero and also considering negative values since byte counts can sometimes be negative in contexts like disk usage differences.\n\nImplementing the formatting logic... Finishing the size formatting logic with precision handling based on the value magnitude, then defining the suffix arrays for both binary and decimal unit systems. Moving on to implement a column layout function that determines the optimal number of columns by iterating from maximum down to one.\n\nNow I'm calculating row counts and column widths, checking if the total width fits within the terminal width by summing individual column widths plus spacing between them.\n\nBuilding the 2D grid structure...\n\nIf the layout doesn't fit, I fall back to a single-column format where each entry gets its own row.\n\nNow I'm thinking about the spacing between columns—GNU ls typically uses at least 2 spaces as a gap, so I'll set that as a constant. I also need padding functions that handle Unicode properly by counting runes instead of bytes, so strings with multi-byte characters get padded correctly.\n\nLooking back at the task requirements, I see there's a distinction between the `Columns` function for grid-based layout and what's needed for tabular output like `wc` and `du`. The requirement asks for column-aligned tabular output where each row has the same columns, numbers are right-aligned, text is left-aligned, and column widths stay consistent across all rows.\n\nBut the PRD specifically defines `Columns`, `PadRight`, and `PadLeft` functions—it doesn't mention a separate table formatting function. I need to check whether the tabular alignment requirement should be implemented as part of the existing function signatures or if there's a different approach expected.\n\nLooking at the acceptance criteria, AC3 asks for a function that produces column-aligned tabular output with right-aligned numerics and consistent widths. The `Columns` function handles the grid layout, while `PadLeft` and `PadRight` provide the alignment primitives—so together they should satisfy the requirement. For tools like `wc` and `du`, callers would use these functions to build their formatted output. Looking at the PRD more carefully, R1.1 specifically calls for a `Columns` function to handle multi-column grid layout like `ls` uses, while R1.2 provides the padding primitives. I think the cleanest approach is to implement those three functions—`Columns`, `PadRight`, and `PadLeft`—and then build a `FormatTable` or `AlignColumns` function on top that handles the tabular output with right-aligned numbers and left-aligned text that `ls -l` style commands need. I need to follow the PRD specifications exactly — implementing `Columns`, `PadRight`, and `PadLeft` as the core API, where `wc` and `du` use `PadLeft` for right-aligning their numeric columns. Let me check the acceptance criteria to make sure I'm building the right thing.\n\nAC3 asks for a function that produces fully column-aligned tabular output with right-aligned numeric fields and consistent widths, which is more than just exporting individual padding functions. I should create a convenience function like `FormatRows` that takes rows and alignment specifications, then applies the appropriate padding to each column based on its maximum width.\n\nDefining the alignment type and FormatRows function... I'm realizing that task R2 explicitly requires accepting rows and columns and producing formatted output with consistent column widths, which justifies creating a FormatTable function that computes the maximum width per column, applies appropriate padding based on alignment, and joins fields with spaces — this directly addresses the wc output format where each column maintains uniform width.\n\nNow I'm setting up the implementation: defining a columnGap constant matching GNU ls's 2-space separator, then writing out the complete code starting with the package documentation.\n\nWriting the format package implementation... D3.\n\n### size.go\nHumanSize + HumanSizeOpts\n\n### columns.go\nColumns, PadRight, PadLeft, FormatTable with Alignment\n\nI need to verify the API matches the PRD exactly. It specifies `Columns(entries []string, termWidth int) [][]string`, `PadRight(s string, width int) string`, `PadLeft(s string, width int) string`, `HumanSize(bytes int64, opts HumanSizeOpts) string`, and `HumanSizeOpts struct { Binary bool }`. \n\nFor AC3, which requires column-aligned tabular output with right-aligned numeric fields, I think the cleanest approach is to implement the PRD functions as specified and add a simple FormatTable helper — that way the utilities can compute their own column widths and apply padding individually, which is how Unix tools typically work.\n\nActually, rereading the PRD non-goals, it explicitly says the package provides only primitives, not a full table library. And looking at AC3 again, `Columns` itself produces column-aligned tabular output, so combined with `PadLeft` for right-alignment, that should satisfy the requirement without needing an extra helper function. I'll stick with just the primitives specified in the PRD. I'm defining a helper function that takes rows and formats them into aligned strings with right-aligned columns and space separation. But I'm realizing I need to handle mixed alignment — numeric fields should be right-aligned while filenames stay left-aligned, like in wc and du output.\n\nRather than overcomplicating this with alignment configuration, I'll stick to what the PRD asks for: simple primitives. The Columns function handles the core tabular output, and PadLeft/PadRight give users the tools to achieve right-aligned numeric fields themselves. That satisfies the acceptance criteria without adding unnecessary complexity.\n\nSo I'll implement Columns, PadRight, and PadLeft, keeping the API minimal and well-documented.\n\nNow I need to work through the Columns logic carefully. With 20 entries across 5 columns, I'd have 4 rows per column, distributing entries vertically. I'll compute column widths based on the maximum width in each column, then add spacing between them like GNU ls does.\n\nWriting the implementation... Writing the column distribution function... Continuing with the layout logic...\n\nNow I'm calculating the total width needed by summing all column widths and adding gaps between columns, then checking if it fits within the terminal width. If it does, I build the 2D result array by iterating through rows and columns, populating each cell with the corresponding entry from the input, and returning the formatted grid. If no configuration fits, I fall back to a single-column layout. Finalizing the column layout logic...\n\nI'm settling on a straightforward approach: the loop will always reach `numCols=1` as its final iteration, so I can rely on that as the guaranteed fallback without needing extra safety checks. I'll set `bestCols` to track the best fit and return the formatted result. Building the output structure...\n\nOnce I have the best column count, I'm organizing the entries into rows by iterating through each row and column position, calculating the correct index for column-major ordering. If an index falls within bounds, I add that entry to the row. This handles the edge case where the loop might not find a fitting layout—if even a single column doesn't fit within the terminal width, `bestCols` stays at 1, which is the fallback.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the three files. For doc.go, I'll document which R-numbers are covered—R1 and R3, since color support is out of scope. For size.go, I need to handle the edge case where the byte value is extremely large, considering int64's maximum around 9.2 × 10^18 and how that maps to binary units.\n\nWriting the imports for columns.go...\n\nFor size.go I need `fmt` and `math`. Now I'm thinking about how to handle negative values in HumanSize — the PRD specifies int64 which can be negative, and I should follow GNU coreutils' approach of preserving the minus sign. Looking at the logic, if I use `math.Abs` for the comparison while dividing the actual value, negative numbers will naturally keep their sign through the divisions.\n\nTesting this mentally: -1536 bytes would become -1.5K, and -500 bytes would stay as -500 since it's below the threshold. The decimal formatting also works correctly — I only show one decimal place when the absolute value is less than 10, which handles negatives properly.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the final implementation. I'll make sure to keep the code clean and only use the standard library, avoiding unnecessary imports like math in the columns module.\n\nWriting the format package code... Writing the HumanSize function... Writing the column layout function... Building the grid layout...\n\nNow I'm finishing up the padding functions—PadRight adds trailing spaces and PadLeft adds leading spaces, both checking the rune count to avoid padding strings that are already wide enough. I should verify the pkg/format/ directory exists before creating these files.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2305,"cache_read_input_tokens":48822,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2305},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d6e0889-97d5-483c-8ced-957e4fcc8896","uuid":"61b78b5e-b80f-4e42-9ec9-70b025a1e40c"}
